## Decided — three items to place (Dean's rulings, not proposals)

1. **`AD2` — `[TA]`-only is unsupported on P/D models; both analyzers is the default.** Dean:
   *"you should not use TA only on P/D. The default should be to use both. This can be documented --
   as long as we only have TAdec we cannot use it as the ONLY analyzer."*
   **Documentation, not enforcement**, and the reason is structural, not laziness: analyzer enablement
   is fixed from controller config while roles arrive at runtime from VA specs
   (`VariantReplicaState.Role`), so at the moment the analyzer set is chosen no role is known. A real
   check would be a *per-model runtime warning* on first observing a prefill-roled variant under a
   TA-only config — separate work, not a config-schema addition. **The constraint therefore ships
   unenforced by design**; the addendum records that so nobody later reads the missing check as an
   oversight. C9's dev-guide pass looks like the natural host for the doc line, but placement is yours.

2. **`AD3` — the from-zero PRC work is scoped to `decode` and `both` roles only.** Follows from `AD1`
   with no further argument: every pricing mechanism (retained PRC, sibling inheritance, demand ratio,
   the `FZ-admission` `PRC = 1` sentinel) is a **denominator** intervention, and prefill's blocker is a
   **zero numerator** — `ceil(0/anything) = 0`. All four are exactly equally inert on a prefill
   variant, and no refinement of any of them changes that. This is not a guard anyone needs to write;
   it is a domain statement for the spec. **It does not reopen `FZ-admission`'s mechanism** — it
   narrows what that mechanism is claimed to cover.

3. **`AD7` — `N5` (sat `Cost = 0` for a zero-replica variant) is to be fixed.** Dean: *"2.cost:
   should be fixed."* Root cause is sourcing, not arithmetic: cost is a spec property
   (`va.Spec.VariantCost` is the precedent) but the pipeline reads it from `ReplicaMetrics.Cost`
   (`saturation_analyzer.go:59`), a live-pod-derived type a cold variant has no entry in. Sizing,
   placement and whether it rides PR-2 at all are yours — the addendum authorizes it and says where
   the field belongs, nothing more.

## The scope call that is yours — `AD5`

**`AD5` is the only genuinely open design item, and PR-2 is about to make it worse.**

The combine is safe — `AD4` verifies TA cannot veto sat in either direction (scale-up MAX with TA
contributing an identity zero; scale-down all-live-agree plus a min). **The anchor is not**, because it
takes `RoleCapacities` wholesale from the binding analyzer. When TA binds on a P/D model,
`anchor.RoleCapacities[prefill].TotalDemand == 0`, so `roleDemandGPUs` (`rescale.go:554-570`) returns
`ceil(0/best) = 0` and the prefill role is granted **no fair-share budget** — while the prefill
`wva_required_capacity` gauge also reads 0, so the symptom is invisible in the series an operator
would check.

**The PR-2 interaction is the part I want you to see before `VG-up` ships.** Today `votingResults`
prunes on `Enabled` only, so a stale-but-enabled sat entry still reaches the scale-up MAX and
*accidentally* supplies a nonzero prefill `RequiredCapacity` during a sat outage. `VG-up`'s
`Enabled && Live` hardening removes that entry — correctly. The side effect is that a `[sat,TA]` P/D
model with a stale sat then runs prefill scale-up on TA's structural zero alone, while decode scales
normally: a model that quietly stops scaling half of itself. Relying on a stale value was wrong;
sizing a role from an analyzer that has no model of it is also wrong.

**My recommendation, which is a predicate and not a new mechanism:** when the binding analyzer is not
role-complete for a role, **hold that role** — the same per-model hold already specified for
`bindingAnchor == nil`.

**Your call, and both answers are defensible:** in-scope for PR-2, or recorded as a known limitation
of `[sat,TA]` under sat outage. The window is narrow (needs a sat outage on a P/D model) and PR-2 is
already large, so deferring is reasonable. What I would not want is `VG-up` shipping with nobody
having noticed it unmasks this — that is the whole reason this handoff exists now rather than after
the coder lands.

## Candidate follow-ups — no decision requested, just don't lose them

- **Retention exception (`AD6`) — Dean's own idea, still cheap, still unscoped.** Carry
  `lastPerReplicaSupply` (+ `role`, `lastFittedB`) across TA's idle expiry
  (`throughput/analyzer.go:158-163`). The eviction's two stated motives — bounding memory, avoiding
  false shape-change signals on recreate — **cover neither the price**; the shape hazard lives in
  `observationWindow`/`shapeTracker`, and `lastFittedB` already has an explicit
  "a shape change must not clear this" carve-out that idle expiry deserves too. Covers a **long-idle**
  variant only, never a genuinely new one.
- **One plumbing change serves three items.** `VariantReplicaState` (`saturation_analyzer.go:386-409`)
  is spec/deployment-derived and exists for a zero-replica variant, but has **no `AcceleratorName` and
  no `Cost`** — both live on `ReplicaMetrics:58-59`, a live-pod type, which is exactly why a cold
  variant cannot see them. Those two spec-sourced fields would supply `AD7`'s cost, the retention
  hazard's accelerator identity, and the accelerator identity a sibling lookup would need. Worth
  scoping together **if any one of them is scoped at all** — this is also the same
  `AcceleratorName`-on-a-never-measured-variant gap the reviewer's Finding 47 hit from the other side
  (`fairShareRolePick` unreachable because `available[vc.AcceleratorName]` is empty). One root cause,
  three symptoms.
- **`N7` can be dispositioned** — `AD4` verifies it fails **safe** for scale-down: a role key missing
  from one live analyzer reads as zero spare, hits `needsScaleDownForRole`'s `<= 0` branch, and vetoes.
  Divergent key-sets cause a role to *never* scale down, never a spurious one.
- **Rejected, so don't re-derive it:** TA cross-variant sibling pricing (Dean: *"skip unless really
  cheap"* — it is not: needs accelerator identity, a compatibility predicate, and a provenance
  decision, and without the first it would inherit an H100 price onto an A100 variant), and the
  demand-model ratio (dead twice per role — zero prefill numerator, and the two analyzers' role demands
  are not proportional, so there is no stable per-role exchange rate).

## One thing I did not verify

The two preconditions the reviewer attaches to saturation's tier-2 (own-`CapacityRecord`) pricing rung
— the `engine_v2.go` `scaleTarget == nil` skip and `capacity_store.go:126-128`
(`EffectiveCapacity <= 0 && EffectiveMaxBatchedTokens > 0`). The addendum's residual table credits
that rung with covering `[sat]`-only and `[sat,TA]` non-disaggregated from-zero. **If either
precondition blocks it for a zero-replica variant, that row weakens and the residual widens to include
`[sat]`-only.** Flagged as unverified in the addendum rather than asserted. One read closes it; I did
not want to state it on the reviewer's authority alone.
